2a:T1922,<p>People often think of salads as summer food. But in Vadodara, winter is when the markets are at their best — piles of red carrots, deep purple beetroot, fresh green peas, tuver and lilva, guavas and oranges. <strong>Winter salads</strong> are a delicious way to make the most of it.</p>
<h2 id="why-winter-is-the-best-season-for-salads">Why winter is the best season for salads</h2><p>From November to February, Gujarat's vegetable markets overflow with produce that's sweeter, crisper and cheaper than at any other time of year. Cooler weather also means leafy greens and herbs stay fresher for longer.</p>
<p>Eating seasonally is good for flavour and often for your wallet too. The <a href="https://eatrightindia.gov.in/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FSSAI's Eat Right India</a> movement encourages eating a variety of seasonal, local fruits and vegetables for that reason.</p>
<h2 id="what-and-39-s-in-season-in-vadodara">What's in season in Vadodara</h2><p>Look out for these in your local sabzi market:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Red carrots</strong> — sweet and juicy; perfect grated raw</li>
<li><strong>Beetroot</strong> — earthy, beautiful colour, great roasted</li>
<li><strong>Radish (mooli)</strong> — peppery crunch</li>
<li><strong>Cauliflower and cabbage</strong> — roast them for nutty flavour</li>
<li><strong>Green peas, tuver and lilva</strong> — the stars of winter Gujarati cooking, and great in salads</li>
<li><strong>Spinach and methi</strong> — tender winter greens</li>
<li><strong>Spring onions</strong> and fresh green garlic</li>
<li><strong>Guava, oranges and amla</strong> — for sweetness, tang and vitamin C</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="how-to-make-winter-salads-feel-warm">How to make winter salads feel warm</h2><p>Not everyone wants cold food on a cool evening, especially when the temperature drops in December and January. Here's how to make a salad feel cosy and warming, so it works as a proper dinner rather than a chilly side dish:</p>
<h3 id="add-something-roasted">Add something roasted</h3><p>Roasted potatoes, sweet potato, cauliflower or beetroot add warmth and a caramelised sweetness. Our <a href="/menu/roasted-potato-apple-salad">Roasted Potato &amp; Apple Salad</a> is exactly this idea: tender roasted potatoes and crisp apple together.</p>
<h3 id="add-warm-protein">Add warm protein</h3><p>Grilled paneer, a soft-boiled egg or warm chicken turn a winter salad into a satisfying dinner. The <a href="/menu/classic-chicken-caesar-salad">Classic Chicken Caesar</a> is a reliable choice on a cold night.</p>
<h3 id="use-warming-spices">Use warming spices</h3><p>Roasted jeera, black pepper, ginger and a little chilli all bring warmth. A dressing with fresh ginger and lemon is perfect in winter.</p>
<h3 id="include-grains-or-noodles">Include grains or noodles</h3><p>Noodles or grains make a bowl hearty. The <a href="/menu/asian-noodle-salad">Asian Noodle Salad</a> with its sesame-soy dressing works well year-round.</p>
<h2 id="five-winter-salad-ideas-to-try-at-home">Five winter salad ideas to try at home</h2><ol>
<li><strong>Carrot, beetroot and orange</strong> — grate carrot and beetroot, add orange segments, roasted peanuts and a lemon-honey dressing.</li>
<li><strong>Warm lilva and paneer</strong> — sauté fresh lilva with ginger and green chilli, toss with grilled paneer and coriander.</li>
<li><strong>Roasted cauliflower and chana</strong> — roast both with jeera and chilli, finish with curd dressing and mint.</li>
<li><strong>Guava chaat salad</strong> — guava, cucumber, onion, chaat masala, lemon and a little chilli.</li>
<li><strong>Spinach, apple and walnut</strong> — tender spinach, crisp apple, toasted walnuts and a mustard vinaigrette.</li>
</ol>
<h2 id="winter-salads-and-your-health">Winter salads and your health</h2><p>Winter produce is rich in fibre and vitamins. Carrots provide beta-carotene, oranges and amla give vitamin C, and leafy greens add folate and iron. Most adults don't eat the <a href="https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/healthy-diet" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">400 g of fruit and vegetables a day</a> the WHO recommends — a daily winter salad is an easy way to close the gap.</p>
<p>Winter also brings richer festive and wedding food. A fresh salad at lunch helps balance the heavier meals.</p>
<h2 id="keep-winter-salads-fresh">Keep winter salads fresh</h2><p>Salads keep better in the cool months than in summer, but the basics still apply: dry your greens well, store dressing separately, and add crunchy toppings at the last minute. Our guide on <a href="/blog/how-to-keep-salad-fresh-and-crisp">keeping salad fresh and crisp</a> has more.</p>
<h2 id="winter-salads-for-gujarati-winter-specialities">Winter salads for Gujarati winter specialities</h2><p>Winter in Gujarat means undhiyu, ponk (tender green jowar) in season, and plenty of fresh green garlic. Some of these flavours work beautifully in a salad:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Ponk and peanuts</strong> with lemon, green chilli and sev in moderation — a classic Surti-style snack turned into a fresh bowl</li>
<li><strong>Green garlic and peas</strong> sautéed lightly, tossed with paneer and coriander</li>
<li><strong>Methi and carrot</strong> with a curd dressing</li>
</ul>
<p>These pair well with heavier winter dishes: a crisp salad alongside undhiyu balances the meal and adds raw vegetables to a cooked spread.</p>
<h2 id="winter-salads-for-lunch-boxes">Winter salads for lunch boxes</h2><p>Cold-weather bowls travel well because cooler weather keeps them fresher for longer. For a lunch box, pack the dressing in a small separate container, put sturdier vegetables like carrot, cabbage and beetroot at the bottom and leafy greens on top, and add nuts or seeds just before eating. Roasted vegetables can be packed warm in a separate box if you prefer them that way. Combine with a roti or a small portion of dal, and you have a balanced, colourful winter lunch that's very different from the usual tiffin.</p>
<h2 id="enjoy-the-season">Enjoy the season</h2><p>Fresh seasonal bowls are one of the best things about Vadodara's cool months — colourful, crunchy and full of flavour. Make them at home with seasonal produce, or let us do the work: we deliver fresh bowls across Vadodara from 11 am to 9 pm. See the <a href="/menu">menu</a> or try a <a href="/plans">salad subscription</a> for the season.</p>
